    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 301754\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1055 AM PDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Chances for isolated to scattered storms linger, primarily in \n  northwestern Arizona and the southern Great Basin.\n\n* Elsewhere, cooling temperatures and gusty southwest winds will be \n  the main story.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Saturday.\n\nHigh pressure remains centered over New Mexico while a positively \ntilted trough sits over the Pacific coast. This pattern will \nmaintain dry conditions for most of the forecast area. Enough \nmoisture remains over some of the far eastern sections of the area \nto keep 15 to 30 percent POPs in the forecast over high terrain in \nnorthwestern Arizona and the southern Great Basin. Thunderstorm \nactivity picks up on Monday as a shortwave tracks east through \nCalifornia and Nevada with decent moisture ahead of it. The current \ntiming of this feature should limit precipitation to northwestern \nArizona and Lincoln County, but if the feature slows down, chances \nmay spread further west. Forecast confidence decreases towards the \nmiddle of the week. The GEFS solution still has the Pacific trough \ntapping into moisture from tropical storm Karina, then drawing the \nmoisture towards the southwestern United States. Meanwhile, the \nECMWF still shows a drier solution. Even if the atmospheric moisture \nmakes it to the forecast area, much of it may stay elevated, \nfavoring high clouds rather than widespread precipitation. Will \ncontinue to watch over the next few days. \n\nThe cooling trend continues as heights aloft lower with the incoming \ntrough. Today's highs will be near normal for this time of year, \nwith highs in the low to mid 100s across the Mojave Desert valleys. \nTemperatures bottom out midweek around 5 degrees below normal, which \nmeans highs in the mid 90s to low 100s and lows in the 70s for many \nlocations. Breezy southwesterly winds will continue with support \nfrom enhanced 850 winds between 30 and 40 knots. Surface wind gusts \nreach 35 to 40 mph across much of the Mojave Desert, especially high \nterrain in San Bernardino, Clark, and Mohave counties. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Breezy \nsouthwesterly winds will continue through the afternoon with \noccasional gusts to 25 knots likely through sunset. South to \nsouthwest winds will persist overnight, although the gustiness \nshould subside by late evening. Similar wind patterns are expected \nMonday, except the southerly gusts may begin earlier around 16Z. VFR \nconditions will prevail through Monday with any cloud bases \nremaining 12kft AGL or higher. Any convection in the region will \nremain well east of the terminal area. Temperatures may briefly \nreach 100F this afternoon, but readings should then remain below \n100F through at least midweek. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Outside of the western \nMojave Desert, where winds will favor a more westerly direction \nthrough Monday, southerly to southwesterly winds will be seen across \nthe region this afternoon. Wind gusts of 20 to 30 knots will be \ncommon through sunset, with lighter winds developing thereafter. VFR \nconditions will persist through the TAF period with FEW to SCT high \nclouds AOA 10kft AGL passing across the region. A few isolated \nthunderstorms are possible over far eastern portions of the region \nthis afternoon, although no convection is expected to directly \nimpact area terminals.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Meltzer\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
